# MMC {{ plant_name }} — Lockout/Tagout (LOTO) Standard Operating Procedure
**Document ID:** MMC_{{ plant_code }}_LOTO_SOP  
**Document Owner:** EHS Manager, {{ plant_name }}  
**Version:** v1.0  
**Effective Date:** 2026-01-26  
**Review Cycle:** Annual  
**Applies To:** All employees, contractors, and service providers performing maintenance, servicing, or repair activities at MMC {{ plant_name }}

---

## 1. Purpose

This procedure establishes requirements for the control of hazardous energy (Lockout/Tagout) during maintenance, servicing, setup, and repair activities at MMC {{ plant_name }}. Proper application of this procedure prevents unexpected startup, energization, or release of stored energy that could cause injury to personnel.

---

## 2. Scope

This procedure applies to:
- All maintenance, servicing, setup, and repair activities where unexpected energization or startup could cause injury
- All energy sources: electrical, pneumatic, hydraulic, mechanical, thermal, chemical, and gravitational
- All equipment on Lines 1, 2, and 3
- All shared area equipment (Receiving/Shipping, Maintenance Crib, Chemical Storage, Battery Handling Station)
- All employees performing or affected by lockout/tagout activities
- All contractors and service providers working on {{ plant_name }} equipment

**This procedure does NOT apply to:**
- Cord and plug-connected equipment where the plug is under exclusive control of the employee
- Hot tap operations on pressurized pipelines (separate procedure required)
- Minor tool changes and adjustments that are routine, repetitive, and integral to production (only if alternative safeguards provide effective protection)

---

## 3. Regulatory Reference

This procedure complies with:
- **OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147** — The Control of Hazardous Energy (Lockout/Tagout)
- **OSHA 29 CFR 1910.333** — Selection and Use of Work Practices (Electrical)
- **NFPA 70E** — Standard for Electrical Safety in the Workplace

---

## 4. Definitions

| Term | Definition |
|------|------------|
| **Affected Employee** | An employee whose job requires operation or use of equipment on which servicing or maintenance is being performed under lockout/tagout, or whose job requires working in an area where such activities are being performed |
| **Authorized Employee** | An employee who locks out or tags out equipment to perform servicing or maintenance; trained and authorized to apply LOTO |
| **Capable of Being Locked Out** | An energy isolating device that has a hasp or other means of attachment for a lock, or can be locked out using a lockout device |
| **Energy Isolating Device** | A mechanical device that physically prevents transmission or release of energy (circuit breakers, disconnect switches, valves, blocks) |
| **Energy Source** | Any source of electrical, mechanical, hydraulic, pneumatic, chemical, thermal, gravitational, or other energy |
| **Group Lockout** | A procedure used when multiple authorized employees are working on the same equipment |
| **Lockout** | Placement of a lockout device on an energy isolating device ensuring the equipment cannot be operated until the device is removed |
| **Lockout Device** | A device that uses a positive means (lock) to hold an energy isolating device in a safe position |
| **Tagout** | Placement of a tagout device on an energy isolating device to indicate that the equipment shall not be operated until the tag is removed |
| **Tagout Device** | A prominent warning device (tag) attached to an energy isolating device to indicate that the equipment shall not be operated |
| **Zero Energy State** | Condition where all hazardous energy has been isolated, locked out, and verified absent |

